rcq("R01-F-08-2", "Structure / function", "Medium",
    "The example of the toss 'described down to the exact force and angle of the flick' (paragraph 3) serves to",
    "show that the propensity view meets its own form of the problem that the choice of class raised for frequencies.",
    [("show that a coin toss is really deterministic, so that its chance of heads is not truly one half.",
      "The author does not say the 'fair coin' chance is wrong; paragraph 4 says that description is 'the relevant one' for a gambler. The example shows dependence on description, not that one half is false."),
     ("illustrate how a physicist with suitable equipment can measure the propensity of any set-up directly.",
      "Paragraph 3 says it is 'unclear what a propensity is'; nothing in the passage claims propensities can be measured directly."),
     ("explain why the frequency view cannot assign any chance to a coin that is tossed only once.",
      "That difficulty is raised in paragraph 2 about the frequency view; the flick example concerns the propensity view.")],
    "Paragraph 3 says the propensity view 'does not escape the problem it was meant to solve', because 'The tendency of a set-up depends on how the set-up is described'. The two descriptions of one toss give two propensities, just as two classes gave two frequencies.",
    "Reading the near-certain propensity as the author's verdict that the coin's true chance is not one half.",
    "The example's role depends on seeing the parallel with the reference class problem of paragraph 2.",
    cognitive="Analysis")

rcq("R01-F-08-3", "Application / analogy", "Medium",
    "Which of the following is most closely analogous to the account of chance given in paragraphs 4 and 5?",
    "A hill's height is definite once one says whether it is measured from sea level or from the plain below.",
    [("The beauty of a painting depends on the taste of whoever happens to be looking at it.",
      "This makes the property depend on the observer's taste, which is the subjectivity paragraph 5 says relativity to a description does not involve."),
     ("The age of a fossil can be estimated, but never exactly, because every dating method carries some error.",
      "This is about uncertainty in measurement; the author's point is that chance is fixed once the specification is fixed, not that it is hard to measure."),
     ("The colour of an object is the one it shows under the single lighting that best reveals its true nature.",
      "This posits a single privileged condition, which is exactly what paragraph 5 says the account gives up.")],
    "Paragraphs 4 and 5 say chance is relative to a specification, the question is 'incomplete' without one, and 'Once the specification is fixed, the chance is fixed too'. The hill's height has the same structure: relative to a baseline, and objective once the baseline is stated.",
    "Choosing the painting because it also involves relativity, without noticing that the relativity there is to taste, not to a stated description.",
    "Two distractors each capture one wrong reading of 'relative' (subjective; privileged frame).",
    cognitive="Application")

rcq("R01-F-08-4", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "It can be inferred that the author would regard two doctors who state different chances of recovery for the same patient as",
    "possibly both correct, if each is relying on a different specification of the patient's circumstances.",
    [("certainly in conflict, since the patient has a single chance of recovery that at most one of them has identified.",
      "This assumes a single privileged description, which paragraph 5 says the account gives up; on the author's view the patient has different chances relative to different specifications."),
     ("both merely expressing their own degrees of confidence, since chance is in the end a matter of perspective.",
      "Paragraph 5 says 'relativity to a description is not the same as subjectivity', so the author would not reduce the doctors' statements to personal confidence."),
     ("both mistaken, since no chance can properly be assigned to the recovery of a single patient.",
      "The author treats single-case chances as meaningful relative to a specification, and paragraph 2 notes that such judgements 'guide real decisions'.")],
    "Paragraph 4 says a chance is a property an event has 'relative to a specification of the circumstances', and paragraph 2 lists several classes the patient belongs to with differing recovery rates. Two doctors using different specifications can therefore both be right; paragraph 5 adds that they would conflict only if they adopted 'the same description'.",
    "Assuming that disagreement about a chance always means at least one party is wrong, or overcorrecting into subjectivity.",
    "Requires combining the relativity thesis with the qualification in paragraph 5 about observers who share a description; distractors capture the two opposite errors.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=HARD)

rcq("R01-F-08-5", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "On the author's account, the choice of which specification of the circumstances to use, unlike the chance given a specification,",
    "is settled by the purposes of whoever needs the chance rather than by facts about the world.",
    [("is fixed by nature, which singles out one description of each event as the correct one.",
      "Paragraph 5 says the account gives up 'the idea that there is a single privileged description, the one nature itself would choose'."),
     ("is left wholly open, so that any figure anyone proposes for the chance is as good as any other.",
      "Paragraph 4 says the choice 'must be settled by our purposes', and paragraph 5 says that once a specification is fixed the chance is fixed, so not every figure is acceptable."),
     ("can be settled only by gathering longer runs of observed frequencies for the event in question.",
      "Frequencies are evidence about the chance under a description; paragraph 4 says the choice of description is settled by purposes, as with the gambler and the physicist.")],
    "Paragraph 4: 'What remains open, and must be settled by our purposes rather than by the world, is which specification to use', illustrated by the gambler and the physicist. Paragraph 5 adds that once the specification is fixed, 'the chance is fixed too'.",
    "Taking 'remains open' to mean anything goes, ignoring that purposes settle the choice and the world then fixes the chance.",
    "The distinction between choosing a description and the chance given a description is subtle, and one distractor exaggerates 'remains open'.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)
